#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1440.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134712} :activity/id #uuid "2f70a73d-f273-5cde-aee6-d7f878bd4f5d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048497 :topic/id #uuid "a4923962-d4db-5668-afba-4796b1d25eb1" :topic/math-academy-id 2119} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135065 :tutorial/content "If we're given a root $r$ of a polynomial $p(x),$ then the factor theorem tells us that $(x-r)$ is a factor of $p(x).$ \n\nFor example, if we're given that $x=2$ is a root of the polynomial  $p(x)=x^3-2x^2-x+2,$ then the factor theorem tells us that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBut what if we want to factor the polynomial completely? How can we find the other factors?\n\nThe trick is to use division. Because $(x-2)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $p(x).$ So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n\nNotice that the remainder turned out to be zero. Whenever we divide a polynomial by one of its factors, we *always* get a remainder of zero.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-2x^2-x+2}{x-2}=x^2-1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^3-2x^2-x+2 = (x-2) (x^2-1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^3-2x^2-x+2 = (x-2) (x-1) (x+1).\n$$\n\nNow that our polynomial is fully factored, we can see that the three roots of the polynomial are\n\n$$\nx=2, \\qquad x=1, \\qquad x=-1.\n$$\n\nWe can use this method and the factor theorem to factor polynomials and solve polynomial equations. Let's try a few more examples!" :tutorial/id #uuid "14dc779e-8160-5cc9-bd16-be0961cab37a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3722 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b074fdae-bb92-50f3-b5a4-fd93ed5771e5" :step/math-academy-id 20951 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134714}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133810 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133619 :question/id #uuid "767b530b-c488-5560-8850-a8f2168648e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5760"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57fe64c-23e2-57d1-b7fc-3543c69342f8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049832}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552434}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Polynomial Given a Factor or a Root"} :step/id #uuid "b2d2702a-1972-5ff2-9e6f-978f1f990b1e" :step/math-academy-id 20952 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134738}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133811 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133620 :question/id #uuid "811e52fd-0786-5363-ae33-aeeb1378046b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5763"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "299354d9-f716-512e-b682-d82b97d03775" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051061}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552512}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Given a Factor or a Root"} :step/id #uuid "3638d18c-dfd3-59c3-a62d-2aebf160dc6a" :step/math-academy-id 20956 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134713}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134738 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133825 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133640 :question/id #uuid "194dad70-c5fe-597c-a2a8-bcec1d699595" :question/math-academy-id "e-6205"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3c42bac-2253-5ceb-9b1f-45adfc8a61e9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050334}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552490}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial With Only One Real Factor"} :step/id #uuid "6941479a-e7bb-5c0b-b217-8eaa5c97f126" :step/math-academy-id 23216 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133641 :question/id #uuid "e119ff72-d5a1-57da-90a1-60cc9d15ee87" :question/math-academy-id "e-6206"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3f33-1b17-5b5e-b196-2abab5504f97" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552494}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial That Factors Over the Reals But Not the Rationals"} :step/id #uuid "0dfe8e99-e134-5271-b437-5766e3d8a4f2" :step/math-academy-id 23217}] :activity/title "Factoring Cubic Polynomials Using the Factor Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134000}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069543} :activity/id #uuid "bf2edb5d-ba82-59e5-8293-ed68021af1ea" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049224 :topic/id #uuid "e4d4205c-47d1-5b87-81bd-c01b64870d2b" :topic/math-academy-id 433} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069543 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069955 :tutorial/content "The **Pythagorean theorem**, also known as Pythagoras' theorem, is an important theorem related to right-angled triangles. Let's consider the right triangle depicted below.\n\n![](images/53/5351dc4269f0b0d04a8c94aa939cc32b333471752f009e5775c829896e63afdd.png)\n\nThe theorem can be written as\n\n$$\nc^{2} = a^{2} + b^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\color{red}c$ is the length of the longest side, which is called the **hypotenuse**, and\n- $\\color{blue}a$ and $\\color{blue}b$ are the lengths of the two other sides, which are called the **legs**." :tutorial/id #uuid "3906cd78-408a-5d9b-80d1-8e5892cb9cb1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2378 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d159d6b3-8d4c-5cb7-b994-7c62f80c0558" :step/math-academy-id 10409 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069544}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069544 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068678 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068539 :question/id #uuid "856c3429-0e56-5123-b2bc-76e3f252b404" :question/math-academy-id "e-3072"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6db510b-5976-5c90-8e02-b9f61ff3327b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048868}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069296}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Length of the Hypotenuse of a Given Right Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "250f28c9-e9bf-5949-8505-fc35334043dd" :step/math-academy-id 10410 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069545}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069545 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068679 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068540 :question/id #uuid "9c6ba1f6-f462-5e47-9731-a906732a46cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3073"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42cc9880-912e-5dce-9b93-e6af88a411c4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555823}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Length of the Hypotenuse Given the Length of the Legs of a Right Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "134b7f42-bc95-57d8-908c-8372da83cad4" :step/math-academy-id 10411 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069546}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069546 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068680 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068541 :question/id #uuid "83f7c86a-a646-5f19-bd80-2df7d218a8ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-3074"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91a6a152-8dd3-5439-9d49-096c5284fcd4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048274}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489267}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Length of a Leg Given a Right Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "f2534b51-1cec-58f8-8ea0-10c382e1a685" :step/math-academy-id 10412}] :activity/title "The Pythagorean Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068964}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489375} :activity/id #uuid "5d7d6b48-4082-54df-a38c-264067bb8314" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049224 :topic/id #uuid "e4d4205c-47d1-5b87-81bd-c01b64870d2b" :topic/math-academy-id 433} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489338 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a9cdac2-f621-564f-8709-0203bdf85dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488589} {:answer/id #uuid "60d53825-bf07-5091-90ed-8b078be85249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{873}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488656} {:answer/id #uuid "79751c18-eaa3-5051-9226-7e74700d3de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488710} {:answer/id #uuid "7c96d7b0-33e5-5715-9230-bacb2c9fa936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488719} {:answer/id #uuid "ac564bf3-c6f3-5221-811b-35794c5d512f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{323}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488810}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488710} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a817628-1149-5ae9-912f-5d3dd1012acd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ce23a05a-85d4-5790-9eda-0cc10c821050" :question/math-academy-id "q-5650" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3c5b5a5c9531b6568c78633f5c44c8332cc722c9d88860a4b6bb9c2c336a1683.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $a$ in the triangle shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Here, we know the lengths of the hypotenuse and one leg, so let $b=\\frac{15}{2}$ and $c=\\frac{39}{2}.$ We need to find $a,$ the length of the other leg.\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ {a}^{2} & ={c}^{2}-{b}^{2} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{{c}^{2}-{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the values for $b$ and $c$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{39}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{15}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1\\,521}{4}-\\frac{225}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{324} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8c98aac9-e3f2-5011-8aaa-b9b56e695d7d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489349}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489178 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ae35f5d-fc84-5280-980e-ba21def821dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488563} {:answer/id #uuid "44486489-16d0-571b-ac64-959574132778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488609} {:answer/id #uuid "89a36b84-72a1-56e6-b785-03e44768abb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488745} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f259dd-0c52-5cac-9647-ed39eaed1dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488983} {:answer/id #uuid "fb25e632-b168-5065-ad33-00818427e651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488609} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1d1d5a9-6a7c-5386-ac49-57029f04b961" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0d67eb70-42fa-5852-8a29-377a03af6acb" :question/math-academy-id "q-82392" :question/problem "A right triangle has legs of length $4$ and $8.$ Calculate the length of its hypotenuse." :question/worked-solution "Let $a=4,$ $b=8,$ and $c$ be the length of the hypotenuse. According to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the values for $a$ and $b$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a571d258-25ad-5505-b4a6-44cb1f7c2043"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489375 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03528b79-9066-5700-95cd-a28d264f9a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488475} {:answer/id #uuid "05f4b190-6978-5583-8c36-6d2df2e0ac80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488484} {:answer/id #uuid "0f2d72d2-a8d1-5dd9-8bea-42b1a2417a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488503} {:answer/id #uuid "6291ad98-e54f-5119-bf5f-b501c0c71255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488658} {:answer/id #uuid "9b15c0f3-e9b3-5a77-9b5d-950decf55d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488780}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488484} :answer-field/id #uuid "912e2818-58ec-5b3c-9288-7b141fa8ef9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9ba7727-634b-5b27-90cd-2da161027238" :question/math-academy-id "q-3710" :question/problem "![](images/66/663096ab9ecc1daca22587d94526cbf0db841602ca0b64eaa1a9477ec8a35aee.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $a$ in the triangle shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Here, we know the lengths of the hypotenuse and one leg, so let $b=12$ and $c=13.$ We need to find $a,$ the length of the other leg.\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ {a}^{2} & ={c}^{2}-{b}^{2} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{{c}^{2}-{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the values for $b$ and $c$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\sqrt{(13)^{2}-(12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169-144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e100fe69-e3de-5f66-8bf1-1a8103c6fd5e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489338}}] :activity/title "The Pythagorean Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488455}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088616} :activity/id #uuid "c4ddd229-d7db-5b1a-a201-7aaf8cc88aac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049242 :topic/id #uuid "e60c86a6-3bce-542f-8289-5aacf26e48e4" :topic/math-academy-id 846} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088995 :tutorial/content "So, we know that the polynomial $p(x) = x^3-2x^2-x+2$ can be factored as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-x+2 & =(x-2)({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy dividing both sides by $(x-2),$ we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-2x^2-x+2}{x-2} = x^2-1.\n$$\n\nSince the result of dividing the polynomial $p(x)$ by the factor $({x-{\\color{black}2}})$ gives *another* polynomial, namely $x^2-1,$ we say that $({x-{\\color{black}2}})$ divides $p(x)$ *with no remainder*. \n\nWe can confirm that we get a remainder of zero when $p(x)$ is divided by $({x-{\\color{blue}2}})$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "1471f79a-59cb-50b0-b82f-f640e329bb7c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9720 :tutorial/title "Connection With Polynomial Division"} :step/id #uuid "f91395f3-3117-5229-a814-b5ebada7f90c" :step/math-academy-id 191472 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088617}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088933 :tutorial/content "If we know a *root* of a polynomial, we can immediately determine a *factor* of the polynomial using the **factor theorem**. \n\nThe factor theorem states that if $p(x)$ is a polynomial and $x={\\color{blue}{r}}$ is a *root* of $p(x),$ meaning that \n\n$$\np({\\color{blue}{r}})=0\n$$\n\nthen $(x-{\\color{blue}{r}})$ is a *factor* of $p(x).$\n\nThe converse of the factor theorem is also true. In other words, if $(x-{\\color{blue}{r}})$ is a factor of $p(x),$ then $x={\\color{blue}{r}}$ is a root of the polynomial.\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial $p(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\np(x)=x^3-2x^2-x+2.\n$$\n\nNote that $x = {\\color{blue}2}$ is a root of this polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(2) & ={2}^{3}-2(2)^{2}-2+2 \\\\  & =8-8-2+2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to the factor theorem, $(x-{\\color{blue}2})$ is a *factor* of the polynomial. Indeed, it can be shown that this polynomial can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-x+2 & =(x-2)({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll learn more about factoring polynomials using the factor theorem in future lessons. But for now, we can check that this is correct by multiplying the parentheses." :tutorial/id #uuid "6ffefc48-3af9-5767-83c4-bf215db91563" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3719 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "848515fd-f525-5e59-ac3a-e5f8bb64943f" :step/math-academy-id 20941 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088567}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088617 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087571 :question/id #uuid "01993d51-8a9f-5156-b5cc-2784e93da5aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-5753"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23c4516d-ba9c-5b55-b971-ceeb575d50c3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051061}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559878}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "cd1ba65e-8d34-5dd7-89fc-a3588ee3811c" :step/math-academy-id 20942 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088618}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088934 :tutorial/content "We can also apply the factor theorem to factors of the form $(ax-b).$\n\nFor example, suppose that $(2x-3)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x).$ Then, we can immediately obtain a root of $p(x)$ by setting the factor equal to $0$ and solving for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the factor theorem, it follows that \n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\nWe can summarize this more general form of the factor theorem as follows:\n\n- If $({\\color{red}a}x-{\\color{blue}b})$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x),$ then $x=\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}b}}{{\\color{red}a}}$ is a root of $p(x).$\n\n- Conversely, if $x=\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}b}}{{\\color{red}a}}$ is a root of $p(x),$ then $({\\color{red}a}x-{\\color{blue}b})$ is a factor of $p(x).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cd34f044-e27c-5559-bb43-7d385325b69e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3720 :tutorial/title "The Factor Theorem for General Linear Factors"} :step/id #uuid "4e0fd1fd-b17d-5ee1-99f9-799218de5423" :step/math-academy-id 20943 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087756 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087572 :question/id #uuid "da4e2c42-894b-5f2a-917b-a5df22f41019" :question/math-academy-id "e-5754"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd5d3a7d-ef95-5d7d-a92a-ce76d6b400bb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559864}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem to Factors With Leading Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "b6addca5-a5ce-5e5a-ba37-b179ad6bd1f6" :step/math-academy-id 20944}] :activity/title "The Factor Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087917}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538013} :activity/id #uuid "d838f33a-4f86-5744-a7fc-eb75d6e74ec7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049242 :topic/id #uuid "e60c86a6-3bce-542f-8289-5aacf26e48e4" :topic/math-academy-id 846} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce67f95-6bea-5f1e-b9d2-1af1c79e9ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537288} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf05eee-7227-5199-af37-fe648f0fca6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537438} {:answer/id #uuid "db03f727-83ad-5c17-8bee-a86151b4ee85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537668} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c51bf4-bc5f-5e98-b7fe-9fe7917b1585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537704} {:answer/id #uuid "eb45be13-00dd-5fd8-b791-31a74d07d791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537711}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537711} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4b04c50-0203-5dfc-9b1a-8237ab5897f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "80bbf2bd-d1de-5b2e-a143-1c03752fb710" :question/math-academy-id "q-112375" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-64?$\n\nI. $x=-4$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(x+4)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(x+4)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+4).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =0 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=-4$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =(-4)^{3}-64 \\\\  & =-64-64 \\\\  & =-128 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-4$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(x+4)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(x+4)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "2e16a771-02ef-5ecb-9a92-67ab9fc2574e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f287a40-81c1-5aaa-a86b-d2b035d996fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537254} {:answer/id #uuid "613837ad-89d1-5321-957d-108904a65b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537421} {:answer/id #uuid "71f3178c-d1ea-5a2a-a611-b33edaa1e845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537452} {:answer/id #uuid "730eb180-4edc-5379-bca0-fecd47808404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537455} {:answer/id #uuid "d3344a98-2866-5a55-94ff-6269de66ca21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537644}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537421} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9167685-de61-5642-a6f3-e2034b8507d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7c34a96f-d461-5b11-9cb5-80d7557e52d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-73777" :question/problem "For which of the following polynomials is $(x+1)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x+2$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{2}+8x+7$\n\nIII. $h(x)=-{x}^{2}+x$" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+1).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x+1)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=-1$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=-1$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=-1$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)^{2}+3(-1)+2 \\\\  & =1-3+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+1)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=-1$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-1) & =(-1)^{2}+8(-1)+7 \\\\  & =1-8+7 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+1)$ is indeed a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=-1$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-1) & =-(-1)^{2}+(-1) \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "3825af64-8557-55d7-8fbb-b760b9c6080d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538003}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538013 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002539d9-f83f-5f8a-821f-f91ec24993f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537223} {:answer/id #uuid "17a8904d-7494-54a8-9cf6-42abe4eadb2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537279} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc2e5c4-3531-5d25-8436-a4eb1e7ccd7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537445} {:answer/id #uuid "b074b252-d948-500c-8a9b-b0d2c27004f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537567} {:answer/id #uuid "f2645d9f-4118-565c-be24-6f9684808038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537729}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537567} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b70b6a-3c22-5376-9d31-4b93eb63027c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6b8e59d5-6b05-50ce-8455-191b0c2a0b7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-73776" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{2}+5x+4?$\n\nI. $x=4$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-4).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-4 & =0 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=4$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & ={4}^{2}+5(4)+4 \\\\  & =16+20+4 \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=4$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(x-4)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(x-4)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} :step/id #uuid "43326e46-5e1e-5d89-9515-b93fb570546c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538007}}] :activity/title "The Factor Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537218}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559924} :activity/id #uuid "17431833-f890-5a9b-bd60-79825d278655" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049242 :topic/id #uuid "e60c86a6-3bce-542f-8289-5aacf26e48e4" :topic/math-academy-id 846} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559896 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2c79867-73d6-5cc0-a3af-25cf7ccb5cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559495} {:answer/id #uuid "c845ff5b-3e9e-5056-902c-a2214e1aa90b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559503} {:answer/id #uuid "d069d041-5456-57d8-b6c8-da7c7f8df610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559520} {:answer/id #uuid "f02ef9f3-2866-5904-be26-7b7de039fb1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559599} {:answer/id #uuid "fce13d87-4fc4-577a-a664-6cc3884100f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559599} :answer-field/id #uuid "c70a0b59-1c4a-5aa7-bb33-d1a6a216cf95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e986899c-1ec0-569d-94ba-d6811b53879c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73779" :question/problem "For which of the following polynomials is $(x-2)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)={x}^{3}-4x$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{2}+4x+5$\n\nIII. $h(x)={x}^{2}-4x+4$" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-2).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=2$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=2$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=2$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & ={2}^{3}-4(2) \\\\  & =8-8 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x-2)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=2$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={2}^{2}+4(2)+5 \\\\  & =4+8+5 \\\\  & =17\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is not a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=2$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(2) & ={2}^{2}-4(2)+4 \\\\  & =4-8+4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only .\""} :step/id #uuid "05656f4c-c5e5-5079-9eb0-b865d0bab77a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559912}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559912 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016050f4-b327-51eb-ac59-6cdb4f2d394b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559017} {:answer/id #uuid "1649ec6b-8d70-5b6e-8662-99e140405802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559064} {:answer/id #uuid "80f25a70-a063-50e7-b4ec-df4df125ef08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559338} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a088d6-83f2-526a-b6ad-dd5f6c749f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559492} {:answer/id #uuid "cc39eb67-2c40-56a3-bfba-b0087f7d23c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559511}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559338} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e9c46f9-73fa-5bae-8ba5-d402d0aa2e48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "50aaea5b-0826-518a-8a27-2b0395848ac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73789" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-3x-2?$\n\nI. $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(3x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(3x+2).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{2}{3}) & =3{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}+2{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-3(-\\frac{2}{3})-2 \\\\  & =3(-\\frac{8}{27})+2(\\frac{4}{9})+3(\\frac{2}{3})-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{9}+\\frac{8}{9}+2-2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ then $(3x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "2c87655d-7944-5ac9-9c29-bd3ab5f7338f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559978}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559924 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35e82de2-f5a4-5a17-8312-4f73519d2e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559150} {:answer/id #uuid "47ca42e7-e9b5-518e-9713-80c7cee79696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559203} {:answer/id #uuid "9e82d34b-fe77-5fe5-9e29-97290fc018c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559418} {:answer/id #uuid "c527e597-42e6-5d49-9d63-cfb389529772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559499} {:answer/id #uuid "fec4a528-b0f6-5c07-bfc6-11532012aef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559150} :answer-field/id #uuid "f84d9338-e966-5479-8801-6e8f15658f84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "81cebe4e-1d65-5357-b789-c0fc16951ab5" :question/math-academy-id "q-38040" :question/problem "For which of the following polynomials is $(x+3)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-8x+3$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-x-3$\n\nIII. $h(x)={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x-2$" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+3).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x+3)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=-3$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=-3$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=-3$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =2(-3)^{3}+3(-3)^{2}-8(-3)+3 \\\\  & =2(-27)+3(9)+24+3 \\\\  & =-54+27+24+3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=-3$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+3(-3)^{2}-(-3)-3 \\\\  & =-27+3(9)+3-3 \\\\  & =-27+27+3-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+3)$ is indeed a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=-3$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+2(-3)^{2}-(-3)-2 \\\\  & =-27+2(9)+3-2 \\\\  & =-27+18+3-2 \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "493d11f3-2102-562a-b794-a790e09ff1be" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559896}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559949 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50a01771-cde1-54b6-903b-5be07bb31b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559222} {:answer/id #uuid "7215867e-586e-537b-aa3c-c7a87d387e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559298} {:answer/id #uuid "7b90dcb3-20f2-5889-885c-583181c9f4e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559323} {:answer/id #uuid "a0014fe0-63da-51e9-bf6d-ad45b394652b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559421} {:answer/id #uuid "fedcbdd6-f4a6-5750-a08c-d1f7b5c28221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559298} :answer-field/id #uuid "8508e80e-2237-5e61-952e-da76d1a6dd3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cef78ed2-9af9-556a-8a9d-05a7101331ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-112468" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-3x+2?$\n\nI. $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(2x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(2x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(2x+1).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{1}{2}) & ={(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}-3(-\\frac{1}{2})+2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{3}{2}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(2x+1)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(2x+1)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "89659a65-f3c8-5528-aa9b-c7187752ecec"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559978 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e583ae8-d9fe-52a9-b929-554bdbe54145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559091} {:answer/id #uuid "2e2007b5-5f0d-5777-a12b-059ac6926d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559133} {:answer/id #uuid "9f06c2d4-90c4-5f39-a2ec-6d108a9826a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559419} {:answer/id #uuid "a94df82a-733b-5f27-86b0-983a28681f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559441} {:answer/id #uuid "e8cb940e-d44e-5a8f-988a-50c419452c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559584}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559584} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4b18df5-7e14-5ba9-ad19-fa824c06c4c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c04c2ae6-0944-57e5-bf4e-7441e7fccca7" :question/math-academy-id "q-112376" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{2}+x-6?$\n\nI. $x=3$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-3).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=3$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & ={3}^{2}+(3)-6 \\\\  & =9+3-6 \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=3$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(x-3)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(x-3)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "e5a159e3-2dc9-58e6-b01c-29cc364c394c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559949}}] :activity/title "The Factor Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558996}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088740} :activity/id #uuid "4ee3f6cf-7f54-503a-9d82-660a1ecf09f8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049695 :topic/id #uuid "0bd4af20-8e63-5977-8ad8-a3833c62469f" :topic/math-academy-id 895} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088740 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088886 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to solve the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nx^2 -2x +2=0.\n$$\n\nThis quadratic is written in standard form with $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=2.$ However, its discriminant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{2}-4ac & =(-2)^{2}-4(1)(2) \\\\  & =4-8 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the quadratic equation has no real solutions. However, if we allow complex solutions, we may solve the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(2)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 2\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $x^2 -2x +2=0$ has two solutions, $x=1+\\textrm{i}$ and $x=1-\\textrm{i}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "275a04ea-da37-527f-9ade-d57aa888110b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2827 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "625577f3-63da-5990-aa37-6436fc70cf20" :step/math-academy-id 455706 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088741}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088741 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087828 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087498 :question/id #uuid "2e80a8b3-de45-51f0-9241-a699222da37b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4157"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbe35c7b-8068-5b28-a820-d25e6b62565a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050465}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522811}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots Using the Quadratic Formula"} :step/id #uuid "7641a04f-8f2b-513e-af55-334dff7e186c" :step/math-academy-id 455707 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088742}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087829 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087632 :question/id #uuid "7f7e9924-1c7b-516c-928c-962a4cd5cbcc" :question/math-academy-id "e-7517"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef8dec7d-5154-59ee-aee8-9f068eb6b8f4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390453}] :knowledge-point/title "Rearranging and Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots"} :step/id #uuid "04a3ac7e-6653-5ae7-bf85-4e6eb8db0752" :step/math-academy-id 455711 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088743}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088887 :tutorial/content "We can also solve a quadratic equation with complex solutions using the square root method.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the solutions of the equation $(x-2)^2=-16.$\n\nThe left-hand side is a perfect square. Therefore, we can solve the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-16} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ x & =2\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSometimes, we need to complete the square first before applying the square root method. Let's see how." :tutorial/id #uuid "145efd5f-c374-5133-89cb-39314be19dcd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2829 :tutorial/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots by Completing the Square"} :step/id #uuid "8514fb33-b596-528a-a07d-1f67ad500b05" :step/math-academy-id 455714 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088744}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087830 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087499 :question/id #uuid "af6ecee9-30de-5913-be21-f184d6f9cacd" :question/math-academy-id "e-4160"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c8c1d8-2662-5c15-b084-084f9e930141" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049250}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537959}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots by Completing the Square"} :step/id #uuid "402941da-4dd4-589e-92f9-62b01650ad6b" :step/math-academy-id 455715}] :activity/title "Solving Quadratic Equations With Complex Roots" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087957}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538038} :activity/id #uuid "d8473e55-ba03-5c28-a07f-8b33a1ce3d26" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049695 :topic/id #uuid "0bd4af20-8e63-5977-8ad8-a3833c62469f" :topic/math-academy-id 895} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c5a9dee-19f1-525b-8742-ffc708372643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p+q=-40,\\,x=-4\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537286} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd7ae77-8250-5020-80a4-12b94cebd915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p+q=-10,\\,x=-4\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537320} {:answer/id #uuid "b040fcf5-22f0-58e5-aba7-527d9e48d77e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p+q=-40,\\,x=4\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537563} {:answer/id #uuid "b1727935-a31f-5667-8134-811f238cf74d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p+q=-10,\\,x=4\\pm 6\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537571} {:answer/id #uuid "e295243b-2633-5336-988f-8567cf89ac74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p+q=-40,\\,x=4\\pm 6\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537686}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537686} :answer-field/id #uuid "751718f7-92f5-50ac-87c6-386399d5f32c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "383c50ad-8f55-5e66-be3c-643b265bd68f" :question/math-academy-id "q-66436" :question/problem "The equation ${x}^{2}-8x+52=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "We first move the constant to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+52 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x & =-52\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x & =-52 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2} & =-52 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}-16 & =-52 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-4,\\,q=-36,$ and $p+q=-40.$\n\nFinally, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =-36 \\\\ x-4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-36} \\\\ x-4 & =\\pm 6\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =4\\pm 6\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=4+6\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=4-6\\,\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "98a1bdb7-2495-5817-9fd6-2f09ad4a6055" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538048}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22b3528e-bd13-54b8-90c1-09193176faa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p+q=-30,\\,x=5\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537303} {:answer/id #uuid "3d076420-a779-5e3e-a34a-583c1f93d33d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p+q=-30,\\,x=-3\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537355} {:answer/id #uuid "96c84526-c5b4-5255-bf98-af414fdb1fb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p+q=-20,\\,x=-3\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537514} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d0d590-2ae3-51d3-8da2-43b173b5a68b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p+q=-20,\\,x=5\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537652} {:answer/id #uuid "df58f72d-ec96-5b04-8b62-aec6bafc8591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p+q=-30,\\,x=5\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537678} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cbb9a83-83f5-559b-aa22-cc741328c862" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b2554883-e4a0-51fb-ab55-70bbe918132c" :question/math-academy-id "q-66431" :question/problem "The equation ${x}^{2}-10x+50=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "We first move the constant to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x+50 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x & =-50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x & =-50 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =-50 \\\\ (x-5)^{2}-25 & =-50 \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-5,\\,q=-25,$ and $p+q=-30.$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-25} \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm 5\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =5\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=5+5\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=5-5\\,\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "9d18d958-1ee9-5247-8bd4-5393f772d4ed" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537897 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d5d2c87-1a8f-5972-81f5-e437b77531ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=9\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537325} {:answer/id #uuid "40c2a656-f6ad-5bfb-96eb-0caf8028ad4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\pm 9\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537363} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa26d8b-77e9-5401-b941-393cc5d1b997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537550} {:answer/id #uuid "e380e9d9-befb-5010-b366-7137019f90be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537689} {:answer/id #uuid "e4eb8c58-f6fc-5d10-80b0-051c38578765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-3\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537694}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537550} :answer-field/id #uuid "63c756b9-1e9d-5271-bbd7-583507116b40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "239de5f3-ea6f-59ef-b333-b7c1243259a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-66777" :question/problem "What are the solutions of the equation $(x-3)^{2}=-9?$" :question/worked-solution "The left-hand side is a perfect square. Therefore, we can solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-9} \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 3\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=3+3\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=3-3\\,\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "b62aaa32-0ff5-5a3f-84ad-80c8603564e8"}] :activity/title "Solving Quadratic Equations With Complex Roots" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537219}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055736} :activity/id #uuid "20dc52fd-28a9-5191-a425-32855a28683b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049831 :topic/id #uuid "1726eb44-d776-5df6-8797-69b129ad3167" :topic/math-academy-id 34} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055736 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055976 :tutorial/content "The **magnitude** of a complex number is its distance from the origin. It's denoted as $\\mid z \\mid$.\n\nFor example, consider the complex number\n\n$$\nz=-4+5\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nshown in the complex plane below.\n\n![](images/33/33d0294178c15b883f8f4988e34d7c81244edd9f426e9ed206e10469dbc5abf1.png)\n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid z \\mid &= \\sqrt{(-4)^{2} + (5)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16 + 25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{41}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, for a complex number\n\n$$\nz=a+b\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe magnitude of $z$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mid z \\mid = \\sqrt{a^2+b^2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "df0d2109-2954-5b2d-8b02-98a43f80ac89" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3329 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "332a5684-42f1-5051-9109-e6eecf07792c" :step/math-academy-id 464642 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055737}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054735 :kno